{"content":[{"c":["ACCAD*"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["One of the three cities (Babel, Erech, and Accad, ",{"c":["rsv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},") in the plains between the Tigris and Euphrates Rivers said to have been founded by Nimrod (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.10.10","r":"Gen.10.10","rawR":"Gen.10.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.10.10","value":"Gen.10.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 10:10"},"). “Akkadian” (from Accad) has become a general designation for the Semitic language of Mesopotamia from the days of Sargon (c. 2360 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},") through Assyrian and Babylonian times."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Accad","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Gn 10:10","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.10.10","rawQueryValue":"Gen.10.10","sourceRefExact":"Gen.10.10"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"46ff328fe12794e665c6527ceb65bdfc20cc9d4ac1de3417c25cf78b921d2316","sourceItemName":"Accad","sourceItemOrder":70,"sourceMember":"Articles/A.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Accad","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Accad*","title":"Accad*","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}